      <Faq faqs={[
        { question: "Do online seed banks ship to Maine?", answer: "Most reputable online seed banks (ILGM, MSNL, Humboldt, Royal Queen, Dutch Passion) have been shipping to Maine for years. Delivery is typically 7–21 days for US-warehouse banks and 14–30 days for EU banks. As of 2026-07-23, the federal legal status of cannabis seeds is unchanged from prior years; verify current status before ordering." },
        { question: "What happens if my seed bank package gets seized at customs?", answer: "Reputable seed banks have stealth-shipping protocols and reship or refund on seizure. ILGM, MSNL, and Seed Connect all advertise reship guarantees. The risk is non-zero but historically low for small personal orders. Banks that ship from US warehouses (ILGM, Seed Connect, Humboldt) avoid the customs interception issue entirely." },
        { question: "Should I buy seeds or clones for my first Maine grow?", answer: "For first-time Maine growers, seeds are almost always the better choice. Seeds are easier to transport and store, you can see the germination rate before committing, and you don't risk bringing in pest or disease from another cultivator's mother plant. Clones are faster (4–6 weeks head start) but require more care on arrival and a clean environment. See the Maine clones vs seeds guide for the full decision matrix." },
        { question: "Do I need feminized seeds?", answer: "For a small home grow where every plant matters (six mature per adult 21+ is a hard cap), feminized seeds ensure every plant is a flower-producing female. Regular (non-feminized) seeds are ~50% male, which means half your plant count is wasted on plants you'll have to cull. For first-time growers, the $5–$15 premium per feminized seed is well worth it. For experienced growers running large rotations from regular seed and culling males, regular seed makes sense." },
        { question: "Should I buy autoflower seeds for Maine?", answer: "For most Maine home grows, autoflower is the safer choice. Maine's 110–160 day outdoor window is short for photoperiod varieties, which need 8–10 weeks of flower that won't start until late summer. Autoflower ignores photoperiod and flowers on its own schedule, typically 60–80 days from germination, which fits Maine's window. Trade-off: lower per-plant yield, no training possible. See the autoflower vs feminized Maine guide for the full comparison." }
      ]} />
